    "source": "For example, in 1961, Bobby Lewis’s Tossin’ and Turnin’ spent 7 weeks at #1.",
    "why": "The data records Tossin' And Turnin' at Billboard peak 1, but it has no weeks-at-number-one field, so the seven-week duration cannot be computed."
  },
  {
    "source": "Meanwhile, Etta James’ debut album dropped the same year, with At Last peaking on Billboard at #68.",
    "why": "The data gives At Last a Billboard peak of 47, not 68, so the story's number does not reproduce."
  },
  {
    "source": "Music historians will regard Bobby Lewis as a pioneer in rock and roll and R&B, yet whatever led to Tossin’ and Turnin’s popularity in 1961 has faded over time. His music, for countless reasons, didn’t persevere in the same way as Etta James’.",
    "why": "The present-day playcounts do differ in the direction described (At Last 10,992,022 versus Tossin' And Turnin' 140,605), but the story's stated premise that At Last peaked at #68 is contradicted by the data (peak 47), so the finding as stated is not reproduced."
  },
  {
    "source": "Oasis, Wonderwall 34,342,877 plays 1996",
    "why": "The data gives Wonderwall 34,352,877 plays, the value used in the story's own top-5 table, so this chart-1 tooltip value differs by 10,000 and does not reproduce."
  },
  {
    "source": "Since 1992, these songs have slowly faded from culture with the incoming generation, whereas Nirvana’s music has not only maintained its popularity, but augmented it.",
    "why": "all_data.csv contains only one 2014 playcount per track and no time series, so a trajectory since 1992 cannot be computed."
  },
  {
    "source": "Today, one track in particular has the most interesting trajectory: OneRepublic’s Counting Stars. Counting Stars never had breakout success following its release, yet it has managed to stay in the cultural zeitgeist for more than a year after its peak popularity.",
    "why": "2013_aggregate_counts.csv has only anonymous personal_id series and daily sum(plays); it contains no track names or mapping, so Counting Stars cannot be identified and its peak date and duration cannot be computed."
  },
  {
    "source": "Today in 2015, both tracks are at parity with Royals and Get Lucky, and you’d probably never guess it.",
    "why": "2013_aggregate_counts.csv contains no track names or track-id mapping, so Young and Beautiful, Do I Wanna Know, Royals and Get Lucky cannot be identified in the anonymous series and their parity cannot be computed for those named tracks."
  },
  {
    "source": "HIGHER PLAYCOUNT PLATEAU, FUTURE TIMELESSNESS ... NOT A HUGE SUCCESS, BUT STEADY GROWTH ... STEEPER DECLINE",
    "why": "These annotations attach to named 2013 tracks, but the 2013 file has no track names, so the trajectories cannot be assigned to the named songs."
  },
  {
    "source": "This song barely charted on Billboard.",
    "why": "The data gives Don't Stop Believin' a Billboard peak of 9, but 'barely charted' is a qualitative judgment and the data contains no chart-run or weeks data with which to quantify it."
  },
  {
    "source": "when it was released in 1992",
    "why": "The data dates Smells Like Teen Spirit to 12/07/1991 and the story's own chart labels it 1991; the 1992 release year does not reproduce, so the top-90s finding is kept without that year."
  }
]

